<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cenél nEógain</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cenél nEógain were a dynasty of the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ui-neill">Uí Néill</a> in Ireland, claiming descent from Eógan, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niall-of-the-nine-hostages">Niall of the Nine Hostages</a>. From their royal centre at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grianan-of-aileach">Grianan of Aileach</a> they dominated the kingdom of Ailech, held the high kingship in alternation with the southern Uí Néill and expanded into mid-Ulster. Their leading branches were the Mac Lochlainn and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/o-neill-dynasty">Ua Néill</a>, ancestors of the O'Neills of Tyrone.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Cenél nEógain, or Kinel-Owen, the kindred of Owen, were a branch of the Northern Uí Néill who claimed descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eogan-mac-neill">Eógan mac Néill</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niall-of-the-nine-hostages">Niall of the Nine Hostages</a>. Their first power base was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inishowen">Inishowen</a> peninsula, with Ailech as their capital. The Cenél mBinnigh, descended from Eógan's son Eochu Binneach, were the first of the kindred to advance out of Inishowen, bypassing the Ciannachta and driving several Airgiallan clans, among them the Ui Tuirtri and Fir Li, east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-bann">River Bann</a>. The Cenél Feargusa followed them towards Tullyhogue and became the vanguard of the O'Neills.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under pressure from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cenel-conaill">Cenél Conaill</a>, the kindred spread east into what are now Tyrone and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derry">Londonderry</a>, pushing aside the Cruithin east of the Bann and encroaching on the Airgiallan tribes west of Lough Neagh. By the 11th century their seat had moved from Ailech to Tullyhogue near Cookstown. When the Cenél Conaill took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inishowen">Inishowen</a> in the 12th century it mattered little, for the Cenél nEógain had by then built the over-kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tir-eoghain">Tír Eoghain</a>, the Land of Owen, whose name survives in County Tyrone. At their height they held much of Tyrone and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-donegal">Donegal</a>, Fermanagh, Londonderry, Monaghan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armagh">Armagh</a>.</p><p>The O'Neills and MacLaughlins of Clann Néill, named after Néill Caille, were the two most powerful septs, until the defeat of the MacLaughlins by the O'Neills in 1241 left the O'Neills dominant. A branch, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clandeboye-oneills">Clandeboye O'Neills</a>, took its name from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aodh-buidhe-oneill">Aodh Buidhe O'Neill</a>, the last king to be styled King of Ailech, and after the collapse of the Earldom of Ulster under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-bruce">Edward Bruce</a>'s invasion took control of most of eastern Ulster.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Many lesser septs traced themselves to Eógan's sons and grandsons. The Cenél Fearadhaigh controlled much of Tyrone by the 12th century and reached deep into Fermanagh until the Maguires broke their power there in the mid-14th century. The Clann Conchúir Magh Ithe moved into County Londonderry and ruled what became O'Cahan Country, and their offshoot the O'Cahans of the Route fought a long rivalry with the Hiberno-Norman MacQuillans. The O'Donnells forced the Cenél Moen across the River Foyle in the 14th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Branches of the Cenél nEógain” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom was dominated by the <b>Cenél nEógain</b>, who displaced the Cenél Conaill from the kingship in the 8th century and alternated the high kingship with the southern Uí Néill.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-ailech">Kingdom of Ailech</a></li></ul></section>
